-- title:
--   Corollary 12 — tracking, and convergence of the cost along the iterates (corrected: $\Lambda^*\in[\alpha\underline C,\alpha\overline C]$)
-- statement:
--   Let Assumptions 1 to 3 and 5 hold, and let $\{\theta_m\}$ be generated by the algorithm in one of the two regimes of Theorem 1, with target $\Lambda_\theta$ in the regime of Theorem 1(ii) and $\Lambda^{(M)}_\theta$ in that of Theorem 1(i). Let $\underline C\le C_\theta\le\overline C$ be uniform bounds on the cost. Then, with probability $1$,
--   $$\lim_{m\to\infty}\widetilde\Lambda_m-\Lambda_{\theta_m}=0,$$
--   and $\lim_{m\to\infty}\Lambda_{\theta_m}=\Lambda^*$ for some constant $\Lambda^*\in[\alpha\underline C,\alpha\overline C]$, which depends on the sample path.
--
--   This is the conclusion of Step I of the proof of Theorem 1: the estimate tracks the cost, and the cost converges.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: Λ* ∈ [−αC̲, αC̄] → Λ* ∈ [αC̲, αC̄]; reason: the printed interval is empty for C ≡ −1 with C̲ = C̄ = −1 on the two-state chain with entries 1/2, where Λ_θ ≡ −α lies outside it; Lemma 12 gives Λ_θ ∈ [αC̲, αC̄]
--   - reading: for some constant Λ* → Λ* chosen after the sample path ω; reason: the proof is pathwise, and the limit may depend on the path
--   - generalization: stated in the setting of Theorem 1(ii) for rule (11) → both regimes of Theorem 1, with the target Λ or Λ^(M), and both rules (11) and (12); reason: App. B, p.21 says the proof of (i) is the same argument and Remark 2, p.25 reduces (12) to (11); the printed case is the instance of regime (ii) with rule (11), and the goal needs all of them
--   - reading: t₀ = 0 with t_m the m-th visit to x*, and a step-size sequence γ_m → Φ₀ = x* almost surely, and γ_m > 0; reason: Step 0 (p.24) takes Φ_{t_m} = x* for every m including 0; Theorem 1(ii) writes 1/γ_m^β
--   - encoding: the adaptive chain and the sequence θ_m → IsRun gives the law of each step given the whole prefix, and θ_m is the fold of the update at the m-th return; reason: the chain is not the Markov chain of one θ; the fold is the recursion on the observed cycles
--   - reading: bounded functions and bounded derivatives in Assumptions 2 and 3 → bounds uniform in θ, with no continuity of the second derivative; reason: Lemma 12's proof and Lemma 17 need uniform bounds; the page says twice differentiable, not C²
--   - generalization: M_m := sup{N_i : N_i ≤ 1/γ_m^β}, undefined when that set is empty → M_m is any value above 1 where the set is empty; reason: the set is empty for at most finitely many m, and every way of filling that case is covered
--   - reading: M with no lower bound in the statement → M > 1; reason: §5.2, p.8 defines g^(M), f^(M) and Λ^(M) only for M > 1
--   - encoding: irreducible and aperiodic → Matrix.IsPrimitive; reason: for a stochastic matrix on a finite type these are the same condition; no instance changes
--   - encoding: expectations over the first return time τ_{x*} → sums over finite excursions, in ℝ≥0∞ or as a real sum with its summability asserted; reason: Assumption 1 makes τ_{x*} finite almost surely, so the values agree and +∞ stays representable
--   - encoding: Assumption 4 with a supremum over θ → Assumption 4 with a summable majorant d; reason: the differences are nonnegative by Corollary 14, so the conditions are equivalent, and no real supremum of an unbounded family arises
--   - reading: N_i ↑ ∞ in Assumption 4 → N monotone (nondecreasing), tending to ∞, with N_i > 1 for every i; reason: ↑ ∞ means nondecreasing to ∞; §5.2 (p.8) defines Λ^(M) only for M > 1, so the terms Λ^(N_i) of Assumption 4 presuppose N_i > 1
-- source:
--   Moharrami, Murthy, Roy & Srikant, A Policy Gradient Algorithm for the Risk-Sensitive Exponential Cost MDP, arXiv:2202.04157v2, Corollary 12, p.33; Lemma 12, p.37

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RiskExpPG_Chain
import Definitions.Def_RiskExpPG_Model
import Definitions.Def_RiskExpPG_Algorithm
open Filter Topology MeasureTheory
open scoped ENNReal

namespace RiskExpPG

/-- **Corollary 12** (arXiv:2202.04157v2, p.33), with the interval corrected. In either regime of
Theorem 1, with `target` equal to `Λ` (or to `Λ^(M)` for a fixed truncation), for any uniform bounds
`C̲ ≤ C_θ ≤ C̄`, almost surely `Λ̃_k - target(θ_k) → 0` and `target(θ_k) → Λ*` for a constant
`Λ* ∈ [αC̲, αC̄]`, which may depend on the sample path.

**Formalization Note** The page prints `[-αC̲, αC̄]`, which is empty for `C ≡ -1`. The page's own
Lemma 12 gives `Λ_θ ∈ [αC̲, αC̄]`. -/
theorem corollary_12 {X : Type*} [Fintype X] [DecidableEq X] {l : ℕ} (m : Model X l)
    (hm : m.IsValid) (h1 : m.Assumption1) (h2 : m.Assumption2) (h3 : m.Assumption3)
    (cfg : Config l) (hcfg : cfg.IsValid) (target : Param l → ℝ) (hreg : cfg.Regime m target)
    {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (μ : Measure Ω) (Φ : ℕ → Ω → X) (hrun : IsRun m cfg μ Φ)
    (Clow Chigh : ℝ) (hC : ∀ θ x, Clow ≤ m.C θ x ∧ m.C θ x ≤ Chigh) :
    ∀ᵐ ω ∂μ,
      Tendsto (fun k => m.lamAt cfg (fun i => Φ i ω) k - target (m.thetaAt cfg (fun i => Φ i ω) k))
        atTop (𝓝 0) ∧
      ∃ Λstar ∈ Set.Icc (m.α * Clow) (m.α * Chigh),
        Tendsto (fun k => target (m.thetaAt cfg (fun i => Φ i ω) k)) atTop (𝓝 Λstar) := by sorry

end RiskExpPG
